<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corpus Christi (feast)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1246">1246</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Feast of Corpus Christi, Corpus Christi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corpus Christi is a Christian feast honouring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, celebrated on the Thursday after Trinity Sunday. It began at Liège in 1246 after the visions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-cornillon">Juliana of Cornillon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> extended it to the whole Church in 1264. It is marked by processions with the consecrated host, and Orvieto Cathedral is linked to it through the miracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Bolsena</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast.jpg" alt="Corpus Christi (feast)" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Emil Doepler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Emil_Doepler_Fronleichnamsprozession.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The feast resulted from some forty years of work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-cornillon">Juliana of Liège</a>, a Norbertine canoness born in 1191 or 1192, who had been raised by the Augustinian nuns of Mont-Cornillon. She reported a first vision of Christ in 1208, in which she was told to plead for a feast of Corpus Christi, and kept it secret for about twenty years. She then petitioned the Dominican Hugh of St-Cher and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-thourotte">Robert of Thourotte</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Liège, who in 1246 ordered the feast kept each year in his diocese on the Thursday after Trinity Sunday. The first celebration was at St Martin's Church in Liège.</p><p>In 1251 Hugh of St-Cher, as Cardinal-Legate, found the feast unobserved at Liège and reinstated it, and the next year he established it across his jurisdiction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Jacques Pantaléon</a>, who had served as archdeacon at Liège, became Pope Urban IV and in 1264 made the feast universal for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> by the bull Transiturus de hoc mundo. The bull gave as the chief reason that so many other themes crowded Maundy Thursday that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> was almost lost sight of.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Tradition says that Urban IV was moved by a procession to Orvieto in 1263 after a bleeding host was witnessed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Bolsena</a>. Scholars have questioned this, noting problems in dating the miracle, whose tradition begins in the 14th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>, at Orvieto from 1259 to 1265, is credited with the office and Mass of the feast, including the hymn Pange Lingua.</p><p>The feast was not widely kept for half a century. It became truly universal only after the bull was included in the Clementines, promulgated in 1317 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>. The central observance is a procession of the Blessed Sacrament in a monstrance, followed by Benediction, with flower wreaths on doors and windows. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> it was also a time for mystery plays: those at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> were performed on Corpus Christi Day for some 200 years until the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the feast was suppressed in many Protestant churches, especially Calvinist ones, because it affirmed the real presence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> condemned processions with the consecrated elements as vain idolatry, yet Lutheran calendars kept the feast until about 1600, and it continues in some Lutheran churches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> abolished it in 1548, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> has since allowed a Day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanksgiving">Thanksgiving</a> for the Institution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Holy Communion</a> on the same Thursday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Feast of Corpus Christi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Feast of Corpus Christi</b> began in Liège, instituted by its bishop in 1246 after the visions of Juliana of Cornillon, before Pope Urban IV extended it to the whole church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium&quot; is a medieval Latin hymn attributed to Thomas Aquinas (1225–1274), written for the <b>Feast of Corpus Christi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Missal of the Tridentine Mass, published in 1570 after the council, kept only four medieval sequences: this one, &quot;Veni Sancte Spiritus&quot; for Pentecost, &quot;Lauda Sion&quot; for <b>Corpus Christi</b> and &quot;Dies irae&quot; for the Requiem Mass.</blockquote><a href="/songs/victimae-paschali-laudes">Victimae paschali laudes</a></li><li><blockquote>The cathedral, begun in 1290, was linked to the Eucharistic miracle of Bolsena and the feast of <b>Corpus Christi</b>; Luca Signorelli later painted its frescoes.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-orvieto">Commune of Orvieto</a></li></ul></section>
